%N A399791 Number of positive integers k such that the sum of decimal digits of k^n is k+n.
%C A399791 For every n, a(n) is finite. Indeed, if k has d decimal digits, then k^n has at most n*d decimal digits, hence s(k^n) <= 9*n*d. Therefore any solution satisfies k+n <= 9*n*d, so k <= 9*n*d-n. Since k >= 10^(d-1), only finitely many values of d, and hence only finitely many k, are possible.
%C A399791 A necessary condition for a(n) > 0 is n == 0, 2, 3, 6, 9, 12, or 15 (mod 18). Indeed, s(k^n) == k^n (mod 9), so a solution must satisfy k^n == k+n (mod 9). Checking k modulo 9 and n modulo 18 gives the stated restriction.
%C A399791 The sequence was suggested by Mariusz Pleszczyński, who also computed the initial terms.
%F A399791 a(n) = #{ k >= 1 : A007953(k^n) = k+n }.
%e A399791 a(2) = 5 since the solutions are k = 2, 5, 8, 14, 17.
%e A399791 a(3) = 6 since the solutions are k = 5, 6, 7, 14, 15, 16.
%e A399791 a(6) = 1 since k = 39 is the unique solution.
%e A399791 a(12) = 4 since the solutions are k = 78, 87, 114, 124.
%Y A399791 Cf. A007953.
%K A399791 nonn,base,new
%O A399791 1,2
